    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Both kinds of ceiling fan plug into the same 120-volt household wiring. The difference is the motor. An AC fan runs a conventional induction motor straight off the mains, while a DC fan converts that power to direct current and drives a brushless permanent-magnet motor through an electronic controller. That one change is why DC fans draw so much less power, offer more speeds and run quieter. It is also why they cost more, depend on a circuit board, and refuse to work with the wall speed control you already own.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy a DC fan for any room where the fan runs for hours at a stretch and noise matters: bedrooms, nurseries, home offices and main living spaces. Buy a good AC fan for rooms where it runs occasionally, where you want to keep an existing wall speed control, or where low upfront cost and simple, cheap repairs matter more than efficiency. Neither is better in every room, and many homes are best served by a mix of the two.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason the decision is not automatic is that the benefits of DC are real but unevenly distributed. The watts saved only become dollars if the fan runs a lot. The quiet only matters if someone is trying to sleep or work under it. And the extra speeds only matter if you will actually use them. Meanwhile the drawbacks, chiefly the price premium and dependence on a model-specific controller, apply to every DC fan whether you benefit from it or not.
          </p>

          <p>
            This comparison sticks to the motor question. If you are still working out blade span or downrod length, our <a href="/what-size-ceiling-fan-do-i-need/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">ceiling fan sizing guide</a> covers that, and the right size matters more to comfort than the motor type does. If you want specific models, our <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best ceiling fans with lights</a> roundup includes both DC and AC picks we have lived with.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
