        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            DC ceiling fans are more efficient, quieter and more adjustable than AC fans, and the Energy Star list is dominated by them for good reason. The trade is a higher price, dependence on the maker's remote or wall control, and a circuit board that is harder to replace than a capacitor.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Put DC fans where they run the longest and where quiet matters most, keep AC fans where simplicity, low cost and existing wall controls win, and check the federal label for airflow per watt rather than trusting a wattage figure on its own.
          </p>
